  "id": "Compound::DB00563 - Disease::DOID:5041",
  "verbalization": "Methotrexate, a cytotoxic chemotherapeutic agent, is implicated in the treatment of esophageal cancer through a complex network of interactions. The evidence suggests that Methotrexate, through its relationship with Cisplatin or Carboplatin, ultimately contributes to the development of esophageal cancer. Both Cisplatin and Carboplatin are known to treat esophageal cancer, and Methotrexate is linked to the causation of spinal cord disorders, which in turn are associated with Cisplatin. This indicates a potential pathway where Methotrexate, via its interaction with Cisplatin, may exert a role in the progression of esophageal cancer. The shared ancestry of Methotrexate and Cisplatin as heteroatomic molecular entities further supports the notion of a common mechanism in their interaction with cancer cells. The integrated evidence suggests that Methotrexate may contribute to esophageal cancer through its role in the treatment of cancers that are sensitive to Cisplatin or Carboplatin, highlighting a possible indirect but significant pathway in the development of this malignancy.",
